    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Once upon a Time... – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Once upon a Time...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize a classmate's presentation for a teacher. Communication: Assist in resolving misunderstandings among peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize a classmate's presentation for the teacher, using key points (e.g., "He told a story about a hero who was fighting a monster."). Can assist in resolving misunderstandings among peers by using simple gestures (e.g., says, "The hero is brave." and shows a strong pose). Can help clarify for peers the main idea of a legend (e.g., says, "This story is about bravery." and gives a thumbs-up).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of famous Panamanian legends (e.g., "La Tulivieja," "El Hombre Caimán"). Students briefly recall one legend they remember from previous lessons by completing the sentence, "Once upon a time, there was a ____ who was fighting a ____ in the ____." (e.g., hero, monster, village). Focus on Past Simple and Past Continuous. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to summarize a short story. For example, the teacher quickly narrates a very simple legend (e.g., "Long ago, a brave young man explored the mountain. He encountered a mysterious creature. He fought it bravely and saved his village."). Then, the teacher summarizes it: "This story is about a brave hero who saved his village from a creature. He was fighting it." The teacher models using simple gestures for 'brave' and 'fighting.' Teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of -ed endings in 'explored,' 'encountered,' 'saved,' 'fought,' and -ing in 'fighting.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I tell you *every* detail, or just the *main* ideas?" (Expected answer: Main ideas.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend doesn't understand, what can you do?" (Expected answer: Explain it, help them, clarify.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short dialogue or role-play scenario presented by the teacher, demonstrating a misunderstanding about a legend's plot and how to clarify it. (e.g., "Student A: The hero was running away from the monster. Student B: No, he was fighting the monster! He was brave!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Clarify details in group discussions and present short stories or legends to peers (e.g., "The man was fighting the monster when the village was attacked.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher acts out a short, confusing summary of a known legend. Then asks: "What was the main problem in the story?" (Gist question).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then asks: "Was the hero running away or fighting the monster?" (True/False based on the correct plot). "How did I clarify the misunderstanding?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Summarizing a story's main points; clarifying misunderstandings using Past Simple, Past Continuous, adverbs of sequence (first, then, after, in the end), and vocabulary (legend, hero, monster, village, fight, save, rescue, brave, mysterious).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Summary Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive sentences with blanks about a familiar legend (e.g., "Once upon a time, a brave ____ was ____ a ____. First, he ____ a magical treasure. In the end, he ____ the ____.") Students complete the sentences using provided word banks that include vocabulary like 'hero,' 'monster,' 'fighting,' 'saved,' 'explored,' 'village,' 'witch,' 'disappeared.' Teacher checks pronunciation of -ed/-ing endings. (8 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Clarification Scenarios (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Teacher presents short, confusing statements about legends (e.g., "The ghost was helping the villagers." - when the ghost was actually scaring them). In pairs, students practice correcting and clarifying using phrases like "No, that's not right. The ghost was actually ____." or "I think it was ____." Students use gestures to aid explanation. (8 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Explain the Plot" Chain (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. One student briefly narrates a legend they know (from previous lessons) in 2-3 sentences. The partner then tries to summarize it in 1 sentence for another "peer" (imaginary or actual). If there's a misunderstanding, the first student clarifies. They then switch roles. Focus on "He told a story about a hero who was fighting a monster" and "This story is about bravery." (7 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Legendary Storybook Pitch & Clarification"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Each student has their "Legendary Storybook" project notes/drafts (from the 21st Century Skills Project). One student (Presenter) briefly presents their chosen Panamanian legend and their storybook idea (e.g., main characters, key plot points, using Past Simple and Past Continuous: "First, the brave hero was exploring the river, then he encountered a mysterious creature."). Another student (Mediator) listens carefully. The third student (Teacher/Peer) might intentionally ask a slightly confused question or pretend not to understand a detail (e.g., "So, the monster was saving the village?"). The Mediator's role is to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize the Presenter's storybook idea for the "Teacher/Peer" (e.g., "He told a story about a brave hero fighting a monster near the mountain.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarify any misunderstandings using target language (e.g., "No, the hero was fighting the monster, not saving it." or "This story is about courage!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use simple gestures to emphasize key points (e.g., thumbs up for bravery, strong pose for hero).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students rotate roles so everyone gets a chance to present, mediate, and ask questions. Teacher circulates, prompts for clarification, and notes language use, especially Past Continuous, Past Simple, and adverbs of sequence, along with pronunciation of -ed and -ing endings. (10 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills and target language use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will, in turn, summarize a peer's "Legendary Storybook" presentation for the teacher (who acts as a 'guest listener') in 1-2 sentences. The teacher will then intentionally ask a clarifying question about the summary, and the student must provide clarification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Summary:</b> Can summarize a classmate's presentation using key points (e.g., "He told a story about a hero who was fighting a monster."). (50%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Resolution of Misunderstanding:</b> Can assist in resolving misunderstandings and clarify the main idea for peers using simple language and gestures. (30%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Appropriately uses Past continuous, Past simple, adverbs of sequence, and vocabulary (hero, monster, legend, fight, save). (10%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of inflectional endings -ed, -ing. (10%)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to help my friends by ____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is important to summarize because ____."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel about explaining a legend to someone who doesn't understand it?" (Scale of 1-5)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of summarizing or clarifying was easy/difficult for me today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The "Legendary Storybook Pitch & Clarification" task worked well for integrating the project with mediation skills. Students were engaged in helping each other understand their projects. Some students still struggled with spontaneously forming Past Continuous sentences when clarifying. More scaffolding for specific clarification phrases might be needed in future mediation tasks. The pronunciation of -ed endings still requires consistent practice for some learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concluded the "Once upon a Time..." theme. The next lesson (Lesson 1 of the new theme) will introduce new vocabulary and listening skills for a new scenario, building on the communicative confidence gained in this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>